dish)","dish/orama-dish"],["Peremech","dish/peremech"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Qatlama","dish/qatlama"],["Qazı","dish/qaz"],["Shakarap","dish/shakarap"],["Shalgam","dish/shalgam"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8c/Boortsog_2.JPG/500px-Boortsog_2.JPG","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Brücke-Osteuropa","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boortsog_2.JPG"}},"dish/crab-cake":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/crab-cake","n":"Crab cake","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A crab cake is a variety of fishcake popular in the United States. It is composed of crab meat and various other ingredients, such as bread crumbs, mayonnaise, mustard (typically prepared mustard, but sometimes mustard powder), eggs, and seasonings. It is then sautéed, baked, grilled, deep fried, or broiled. Crab cakes are traditionally associated with the Chesapeake Bay, in the state of Maryland. Although the earliest use of the term \"crab cake\" is commonly believed to date to Crosby Gaige's 1939 publication New York World's Fair Cook Book in which they are described as \"Baltimore crab cakes,\" earlier usages can be found such as in Thomas J. Murrey's book Cookery with a Chafing Dish published in 1891."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-crab-cake"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crab_cake"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Crab meat","ingredient/crab-meat"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-de