liatelle typical of Bologna."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-fettuccine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fettuccine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian-American cuisine","cuisine/italian-american-cuisine",[["Fettuccine Alfredo","dish/fettuccine-alfredo"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Parmesan","ingredient/parmesan"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/cf/Fettucine1.JPG/500px-Fettucine1.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"LPLT","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fettucine1.JPG"}},"dish/flammekueche":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/flammekueche","n":"Flammekueche","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 4 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Flammekueche (Alsatian), Flammkuchen (Standard German), or tarte flambée (French), is a speciality of the region of Alsace, the German-speaking Moselle region, Saarland, Baden and the Palatinate. It is composed of bread dough rolled out very thinly in the shape of a rectangle or oval, which is usually covered with fromage blanc or crème fraîche, thinly sliced onions and lardons."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-flammekueche"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flammekueche"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Crème fraîche","ingredient/creme-fraiche"],["Fromage frais","ingredient/fromage-frais"],["Lardon","ingredient/lardon"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Quick German Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"quick-german-recipes.com","note":"Long-running site by German-born cook Gerhild Fulson (Oma) with traditional German home recipes.","url":"https://www.quick-german-recipes.com/about-me.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Dirndl Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dirndlkitchen.com","note":"Blog of German and Bavarian recipes, from main dishes to holiday baking.","url":"https://dirndlkitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["Adventureland Bazaar","dish/adventureland-bazaar"],["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Bayonne ham","dish/bayonne-ham"],["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Beurre maître d'hôtel","dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish/beurre-manie"],["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Blanquette de veau","dish/blanquette-de-veau"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bouillabaisse","dish/bouillabaisse"],["Bourride","dish/bourride"],["Brioche","dish/brioche"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Calisson","dish/calisson"],["Canapé","dish/canape"],["Cervelle de canut","dish/cervelle-de-canut"],["Cream of sorrel soup","dish/cream-of-sorrel-soup"],["Crêpes Suzette","dish/crepes-suzette"],["Croquembouche","dish/croquembouche"],["Crozets de Savoie","dish/crozets-de-savoie"],["Crudités","dish/crudites"],["Damien Pignolet","dish/damien-pignolet"],["Daube","dish/daube"],["Demi-glace","dish/demi-glace"],["Duxelles","dish/duxelles"],["Egg wash","dish/egg-wash"],["Espagnole sauce","dish/espagnole-sauce"],["Fin gras du Mézenc","dish/fin-gras-du-mezenc"],["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"],["Foin de Crau","dish/foin-de-crau"],["Fraisier","dish/fraisier"],["French onion soup","dish/french-onion-soup"],["French restaurant","dish/french-restaurant"],["Fricassee","dish/fricassee"],["Garbure","dish/garbure"],["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"],["Gugelhupf","dish/gugelhupf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d6/Franchettis_Gasthaus_%2B_Kitchen_-_2021-02-05_-_Sarah_Stierch_06.jpg/500px-Franchettis_Gasthaus_%2B_Kitchen_-_2021-02-05_-_Sarah_Stierch_06.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Missvain","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franchettis_Gasthaus_%2B_Kitchen_-_2021-02-05_-_Sarah_Stierch_06.jpg"}},"dish/fruktsoppa":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/fruktsoppa","n":"Fruktsoppa","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Fruktsoppa is a fruit soup that is typically prepared using dried fruits, and usually served as a dessert dish. The dish has been described as a \"cold fruit pudding.\" It is a traditional dessert in Sweden and Norway. Historically, during the winter months in Scandinavian countries, fresh fruit was generally unavailable, so people used dried fruits for the preparation of various dishes, including fruktsoppa. The soup may be served hot or cold. The soup can be made with one fruit or with multiple fruits; a soup which is made with multiple fruits may be called blandad fruktsoppa, which is Swedish for \"mixed fruit soup\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-fruktsoppa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruktsoppa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Norwegian cuisine","cuisine/norwegian-cuisine"],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Red wine","ingredient/red-wine"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Scandicuisine: Swedish recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scandicuisine.com","note":"Traditional Swedish recipes from Cecilia, a cook from a Finnish-Swedish family on Åland.","url":"https://scandicuisine.com/category/swedish-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Norwegian cuisine","links":[["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Kransekage","dish/kransekage"],["Labskaus","dish/labskaus"],["Lutefisk","dish/lutefisk"],["Risalamande","dish/risalamande"],["Trollkrem","dish/trollkrem"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/3/32/Fruit_soup_%28Swedish_Varma_Koppen%29_%283077175410%29.jpg","w":500,"h":512,"by":"Josefine Stenudd from Gothenburg, Sweden","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fruit_soup_(Swedish_Varma_Koppen)_(3077175410).jpg"}},"ingredient/unagi":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/unagi","n":"Unagi","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Unagi is the Japanese word for freshwater eel, particularly the Japanese eel, Anguilla japonica (日本鰻, nihon unagi). Unagi is a common ingredient in Japanese cooking, often as kabayaki. It is not to be confused with saltwater eel, which is known as anago in Japanese."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-unagi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unagi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5e/%E5%B0%8F%E5%B7%9D%E8%8F%8A%E9%B0%BB%E9%AD%9A%E9%A3%AF_%2849287165332%29.jpg/500px-%E5%B0%8F%E5%B7%9D%E8%8F%8A%E9%B0%BB%E9%AD%9A%E9%A3%AF_%2849287165332%29.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Wei-Te Wong from Taipei City, Taiwan, Republic of China","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F%E5%B7%9D%E8%8F%8A%E9%B0%BB%E9%AD%9A%E9%A3%AF_(49287165332).jpg"}},"ingredient/moringa-genus":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/moringa-genus","n":"Moringa (genus)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Moringa is the sole genus in the plant family Moringaceae. It contains 13 species, which occur in tropical and subtropical regions of Africa and Asia and that range in size from tiny herbs to massive trees. Moringa species grow quickly in many types of environments."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-moringa-genus"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moringa_(genus)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Kapurung","dish/kapurung"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Asparagus bean","ingredient/asparagus-bean"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Pindang","ingredient/pindang"],["Sago","ingredient/sago"],["Shrimp paste","ingredient/shrimp-paste"],["Water spinach","ingredient/water-spinach"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/22/Moringa_ovalifolia.jpg/500px-Moringa_ovalifolia.jpg","w":500,"h":756,"by":"© Hans Hillewaert","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moringa_ovalifolia.jpg"}},"ingredient/duck-eggs":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/duck-eggs","n":"Duck eggs","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Humans and other hominids have consumed eggs for millions of years. The most widely consumed eggs are those of fowl, especially chickens. People in Southeast Asia began harvesting chicken eggs for food around 1500 BCE. Eggs of other birds, such as ducks and ostriches, are eaten regularly but much less commonly than those of chickens. People may also eat the eggs of reptiles, amphibians, and fish. Fish eggs consumed as food are known as roe or caviar."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-duck-eggs"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duck_eggs"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",[["Kumukunsi","dish/kumukunsi"]]],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao",[["Kumukunsi","dish/kumukunsi"]]],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas",[["Kumukunsi","dish/kumukunsi"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Duck egg","ingredient/duck-egg"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3f/Huevo_frito.jpg/500px-Huevo_frito.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Horacio Cambeiro","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huevo_frito.jpg"}},"dish/kurtoskalacs":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/kurtoskalacs","n":"Kürtőskalács","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Hungarian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Kürtőskalács is a spit cake specific to Hungarians from Transylvania, more specifically the Székelys. Originally popular in the Székely Land, it became popular in both Hungary and Romania. The first written record dates back to 1679 and was found in the village of Úzdiszentpéter (now Sânpetru de Câmpie), while the first recipe appears in a manuscript cookbook dated in 1781. Earlier a festive treat, now it is part of everyday consumption. A similar pastry to kürtőskalács is Baumstriezel, originating in the Transylvanian Saxon communities."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-kurtoskalacs"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C3%BCrt%C5%91skal%C3%A1cs"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hungarian cuisine","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Zsuzsa Is in the Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"zsuzsaisinthekitchen.blogspot.com","note":"Hungarian-born home cook's blog of traditional Hungarian recipes.","url":"https://zsuzsaisinthekitchen.blogspot.com/2010/01","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Hungarian cuisine","links":[["Arany galuska","dish/arany-galuska"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chicken paprikash","dish/chicken-paprikash"],["Esterházy torte","dish/esterhazy-torte"],["Goulash","dish/goulash"],["Gulyásleves","dish/gulyasleves"],["Jókai bean soup","dish/jokai-bean-soup"],["Lecsó","dish/lecso"],["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"],["Palóc soup","dish/paloc-soup"],["Pörkölt","dish/porkolt"],["Rakott krumpli","dish/rakott-krumpli"],["Rigójancsi","dish/rigojancsi"],["Sour cherry soup","dish/sour-cherry-soup"],["Speckkuchen","dish/speckkuchen"],["Székelykáposzta","dish/szekelykaposzta"],["Tarhonya","dish/tarhonya"],["Trdelník","dish/trdelnik"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/09/K%C3%BCrt%C5%91skal%C3%A1cs2011.jpg/500px-K%C3%BCrt%C5%91skal%C3%A1cs2011.jpg","w":500,"h":624,"by":"Cyril5555","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%BCrt%C5%91skal%C3%A1cs2011.jpg"}},"ingredient/pistachio-nut":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pistachio-nut","n":"Pistachio nut","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["The pistachio is a small to medium-sized tree  of the cashew family. The tree produces seeds that are widely consumed as food. Pistachios can be used as an ingredient in desserts, chocolate, candy, ice cream, and as a paste or butter. As a tree nut, pistachios are considered a priority allergen that may induce allergic reactions in susceptible people."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pistachio-nut"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pistachio_nut"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Lebanese cuisine","cuisine/lebanese-cuisine",[["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine",[["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Palestinian cuisine","cuisine/palestinian-cuisine",[["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Arab cuisine","cuisine/arab-cuisine",[["Meghli","dish/meghli"]]],["Egyptian cuisine","cuisine/egyptian-cuisine",[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Jordanian cuisine","cuisine/jordanian-cuisine",[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Syrian cuisine","cuisine/syrian-cuisine",[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Anise","ingredient/anise"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Caraway seed","ingredient/caraway-seed"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Pine nut","ingredient/pine-nut"],["Pistachio","ingredient/pistachio"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Baking powder","ingredient/baking-powder"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b5/Pistachio_vera.jpg/500px-Pistachio_vera.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"THOR","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pistachio_vera.jpg"}},"ingredient/chum-salmon":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/chum-salmon","n":"Chum salmon","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The chum salmon (Oncorhynchus keta), also known as dog salmon or keta salmon, is a species of anadromous salmonid fish from the genus Oncorhynchus (Pacific salmon) native to the coastal rivers of the North Pacific and the Beringian Arctic. The English name \"chum salmon\" comes from the Chinook Jargon term tsəm, meaning \"spotted\" or \"marked\"; while keta in the scientific name comes from Russian, which in turn comes from the Evenki language of Eastern Siberia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-chum-salmon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chum_salmon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Onigiri","dish/onigiri"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Japonica rice","ingredient/japonica-rice"],["Katsuobushi","ingredient/katsuobushi"],["Kombu","ingredient/kombu"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Nori","ingredient/nori"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/71/Dog_Salmon_Breeding_Male.jpg/500px-Dog_Salmon_Breeding_Male.jpg","w":500,"h":270,"by":"A. Hoen and Co.","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dog_Salmon_Breeding_Male.jpg"}},"dish/peppermint-bark":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/peppermint-bark","n":"Peppermint bark","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Peppermint bark is a chocolate confection. Generally, it consists of peppermint candy pieces, such as candy canes, in white chocolate on top of dark chocolate, but peppermint bark can refer to any chocolate with peppermint candy pieces in it."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-peppermint-bark"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peppermint_bark"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Candy cane","ingredient/candy-cane"],["Caramel","ingredient/caramel"],["Dark chocolate","ingredient/dark-chocolate"],["Peppermint","ingredient/peppermint"],["White chocolate","ingredient/white-chocolate"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/0/06/Peppermint_Bark_%283197351955%29.jpg","w":500,"h":332,"by":"kake futon from Tokyo","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peppermint_Bark_(3197351955).jpg"}},"dish/shalgam":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/shalgam","n":"Shalgam","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Kazakh cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Shalgam is a traditional salad from Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan. It is made from radishes (shalgam), sweet Bulgarian bell pepper, carrots, onions, garlic and spices."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-shalgam"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shalgam"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Kazakh cuisine","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Radish","ingredient/radish"]]},{"h":"More from Kazakh cuisine","links":[["Beshbarmak","dish/beshbarmak"],["Boortsog","dish/boortsog"],["Chorba","dish/chorba"],["Dimlama","dish/dimlama"],["Kuurdak","dish/kuurdak"],["Naryn (dish)","dish/naryn-dish"],["Orama (dish)","dish/orama-dish"],["Peremech","dish/peremech"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Qatlama","dish/qatlama"],["Qazı","dish/qaz"],["Shakarap","dish/shakarap"]]}]},"ingredient/bivalvia":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bivalvia","n":"Bivalvia","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Bivalvia () or bivalves, in previous centuries referred to as the Lamellibranchiata and Pelecypoda, is a class of aquatic molluscs (marine and freshwater) that have laterally compressed soft bodies enclosed by a calcified exoskeleton consisting of a hinged pair of half-shells known as valves. As a group, bivalves have no head and lack some typical molluscan organs such as the radula and the odontophore. Their gills have evolved into ctenidia, specialised organs for feeding and breathing."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bivalvia"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bivalvia"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Tempura","dish/tempura"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Flammulina filiformis","ingredient/flammulina-filiformis"],["Shiitake","ingredient/shiitake"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Squid","ingredient/squid"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/31/Haeckel_Acephala.jpg/500px-Haeckel_Acephala.jpg","w":500,"h":706,"by":"Ernst Haeckel","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haeckel_Acephala.jpg"}},"ingredient/corncob":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/corncob","n":"Corncob","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A corncob, also called corn cob or cob of corn, is the hard core of an ear of maize, bearing the kernels, made up of the chaff, woody ring, and pith. Corncobs contain mainly cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-corncob"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corncob"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Tinutuan","dish/tinutuan"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Congee","ingredient/congee"],["Cucurbita","ingredient/cucurbita"],["Granulated sugar","ingredient/granulated-sugar"],["Holy basil","ingredient/holy-basil"],["Ipomoea aquatica","ingredient/ipomoea-aquatica"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Salt-cured meat","ingredient/salt-cured-meat"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Tapioca","ingredient/tapioca"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c7/6925Corncobs_in_the_Philippines_Putok_na_Bigas_33.jpg/500px-6925Corncobs_in_the_Philippines_Putok_na_Bigas_33.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Judgefloro","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publ